      "math_academy_id": "e-2477",
      "knowledge_point_id": "49b54258-61d2-549c-94e4-a251f6d2a656",
      "knowledge_point": "Using the Sum and Constant Multiple Rules for Differentiation",
      "problem": "Find the derivative of $\\mathbf f(t) + 2\\mathbf g(t)$ with respect to $t,$ where $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =(4{t}^{2}+9)\\,i-(\\frac{1}{t-2})\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =(4t-1)\\,i+{e}^{3t}\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the constant multiple rule and the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf f(t) + 2\\mathbf g(t))' = \\mathbf f'(t) + 2\\mathbf g'(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivative of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}+9)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{1}{t-2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,k \\\\  & =8t\\,i+\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k, \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t})j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)k \\\\  & =4\\,i+3{e}^{3t}\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f(t)+2g(t))^{′} & ={f}^{′}(t)+2{g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(8t\\,i+\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k)+2(4\\,i+3{e}^{3t}\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k) \\\\  & =8(t+1)\\,i+(\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}+6{e}^{3t})\\,j-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-2478",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b19f5f01-948e-5f44-8016-63073ed31dbc",
      "knowledge_point": "Using the Scalar Function Multiple Rule for Differentiation",
      "problem": "Find $\\big( u(t) \\mathbf g(t) \\big)'$ where ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14077032/assets/9e23d542bfc56d506f5437a35a92c304d618109ef60086e1f5a7a4fee57d8a80.png) and $u(t) = t.$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\big( u(t) \\mathbf g(t) \\big)' = u'(t) \\mathbf{g}(t) + u(t) \\mathbf{g}'(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find $\\mathbf g'(t)$ and $u'(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{t} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)g(t))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(t)g(t)+u(t){g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ -t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+t\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{t} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ -t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -t \\\\ 2t{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ -2t \\\\ (2t+1){e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
